The '''Internationalist Brigades''' ([[Baliscano]]: ''Brigadas internationalista;'' often abbreviated '''BI''') is a predominately [[W:Anarchism|anarchist]] armed organization, which effectively operated as [[W:Far-left|left-unity]] [[W:Paramilitary|paramilitary]] that engaged in direct medical and military internationalism in support of left-wing [[W:Revolutionary movement|revolutionary]] and [[W:Liberation movement|liberation movements]] worldwide, but never for governments. 

It has fought as a [[W:paramilitary|volunteer]] force in several revolutionary conflicts. It first saw action in the the [[W:Spanish Revolution of 1936|Spanish Revolution of 1936]] in support of [[W:Revolutionary Catalonia|Revolutionary Catalonia]] and [[W:Confederación Nacional del Trabajo|CNT]]/[[W:Federación Anarquista Ibérica|FAI]]. Balisca had relatively [[Balisca–Soviet Union relations|cold relations]] with the [[Soviet Union]] as it strongly rebuked the [[W:Bolsheviks|Bolshevik]] government, but the two agreed to collaborate during the [[W:Spanish Civil War|Spanish Civil War]] to support the [[W:Republicans (Spanish Civil War faction)|Republican]] forces.

It was able to circumvent the confederal policy of {{W|Non-Aligned movement|nonalignment}} during the {{W|Cold War}}, bringing together various autonomous leftist factions within the Confederation united by their shared support of {{W|proletarian internationalism}}. The Brigades were largely viewed as defunct following the end of the Cold War, but re-emerged in in 2012 to support the [[W:Autonomous Administration of North and East Syria|Autonomous Administration of North and East Syria]] amidst the [[W:Rojava conflict|Rojava conflict]].  

The Brigades' three-pointed Red Star is the emblem of unity of all progressive forces. It means the world-wide cooperation of Marxists, Anarchists and Republican Democrats. Sometimes this is expressed as the unity in principle of Marxists, Anarchists and Trade Unionists, depending upon context.  

